    <body>
      <sec id="article-24497.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>"Dead space" denotes the volume of ventilated air that does not participate in gas exchange. Two types of dead space exist: anatomical and physiologic. Anatomical dead space corresponds to the volume of air contained within the conducting zone of respiration, which includes the nose, trachea, and bronchi (see <bold>Image</bold>. Anatomic Dead Space). This volume approximates 30% of normal tidal volume (500 mL), resulting in an anatomic dead space of 150 mL. Physiologic, or total, dead space equals the sum of anatomic and alveolar dead space. Alveolar dead space represents the volume of air in the respiratory zone that fails to participate in gas exchange. The respiratory zone comprises respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, and alveoli. Alveolar dead space is negligible in healthy adults.&#x000a0;Therefore, physiologic dead space approximates anatomical dead space. In certain pulmonary diseases, physiologic dead space increases due to impaired alveolar diffusion or ventilation-perfusion mismatch.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24497.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24497.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24497.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24497.s2" sec-type="Function">
        <title>Function</title>
        <p>Ventilation&#x000a0;is the process by which air enters the lungs. Two equations are required to determine the volume of air entering the lungs and the volume reaching the alveoli. Minute ventilation (VE) represents the volume of air entering the lungs per minute. Minute ventilation may be calculated from&#x000a0;tidal volume (VT) and respiratory rate (RR)&#x000a0;using the following equation:</p>
        <p>VE = VT &#x000d7; RR</p>
        <p>This equation demonstrates that total inspired volume does not equal the volume of gas reaching the alveoli, as gas occupying the anatomical dead space within the conducting airways is not included in gas exchange. Determination of the volume of gas reaching the alveoli per unit time requires the alveolar ventilation equation, as follows:</p>
        <p>VA = (VT &#x02212; VD) &#x000d7; RR</p>
        <p>In this equation, VA represents alveolar ventilation, and VD represents physiologic dead space. This relationship indicates that a portion of inspired gas does not participate in gas exchange due to the presence of anatomical dead space.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24497.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24497.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24497.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24497.s3" sec-type="Mechanism">
        <title>Mechanism</title>
        <p>The Bohr equation is used to quantify physiologic dead space within the lungs. Understanding this relationship clarifies the concept of dead space. The equation is expressed as follows:</p>
        <p>VD = VT &#x000d7; (PaCO2 &#x02212; PeCO2) / PaCO2</p>
        <p>In this equation, tidal volume (VT) is the volume of inspired and expired gas, typically approximating 500 mL. Inspired air contains a negligible amount of carbon dioxide. The fractional term (PaCO2 &#x02212; PeCO2) / PaCO2 represents the proportion of ventilation that does not participate in gas exchange. This term reflects the difference between arterial carbon dioxide partial pressure (PaCO2) and mixed expired carbon dioxide partial pressure (PeCO2).</p>
        <p>Gas exchange across the respiratory membrane occurs rapidly, allowing PaCO2 to approximate alveolar&#x000a0;carbon dioxide partial pressure. The value of PeCO2 is consistently lower than PaCO2 due to dilution of alveolar gas with air from the anatomical dead space within the conducting airways, which is assumed to contain negligible carbon dioxide. Calculation of the difference between PaCO2 and PeCO2, followed by normalization to PaCO2, yields the fraction of ventilation that does not contribute to gas exchange. Multiplication of this fraction by tidal volume provides the physiologic dead space.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24497.s4" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The discussion up to this point presumes efficient alveolar gas exchange in the absence of disease. Gas exchange decreases&#x000a0;while alveolar dead space increases in disease states characterized by loss of alveolar function. The most rapid changes occur with sudden reductions in perfusion relative to ventilation at the alveolar level. Such changes are observed in conditions that include&#x000a0;abrupt decreases in cardiac output, hypotension, or pulmonary embolism caused by fat, air, or amniotic fluid. Although vascular obstruction contributes to reduced perfusion in pulmonary embolism, the predominant reduction in pulmonary blood flow results from vasoconstriction mediated by locally released vasoactive substances. Under these conditions, impaired gas exchange at the alveolar level reduces PaCO2. Compensation by remaining functional alveoli leads to a reduction in PeCO2. In the context of the Bohr equation, a decrease in PeCO2 results in an increase in physiologic dead space. Ventilation of alveoli in the presence of inadequate perfusion increases physiologic dead space.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24497.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24497.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24497.s5" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Clinically, both disease states and environmental exposures, including smoking, contribute to increases in dead space. Increased dead space occurs in several pulmonary conditions, including emphysema, pneumonia, and acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). Emphysema is characterized by enlargement of air spaces and reduced diffusing capacity of the alveolar membrane secondary to destruction of alveolar walls. In ARDS, endothelial injury increases alveolar-capillary permeability, resulting in leakage of protein-rich fluid into the alveoli. This process leads to the formation of intra-alveolar hyaline membranes, which impair the exchange of carbon dioxide and oxygen and contribute to increased dead space. Studies evaluating causes of mortality in this condition demonstrate higher dead space values in nonsurvivors compared with survivors. The strongest association between increased dead space volume and mortality risk is observed in patients with ARDS.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24497.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Knowledge of dead space informs the&#x000a0;optimization of mechanical ventilation.&#x000a0;This intervention can increase apparatus dead space due to components of the breathing circuit, whereas placement of an endotracheal tube reduces anatomical dead space by bypassing the upper airway. Capnography provides measurement of end-tidal&#x000a0;carbon dioxide (EtCO2) during mechanical ventilation. Comparison of EtCO2 with PaCO2 allows estimation of physiologic dead space.</p>
        <p>Adjustments to the ventilator, including respiratory rate, tidal volume, and positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP), influence dead space. PEEP may reduce alveolar dead space by improving alveolar recruitment and ventilation&#x02013;perfusion matching.&#x000a0;However, excessive PEEP can increase dead space through alveolar overdistension and reduction of pulmonary perfusion. Despite variable evidence, PEEP remains widely applied in conditions such as ARDS.</p>
        <p>High-flow nasal cannula therapy can reduce dead space by washing out carbon dioxide from the upper airway and minimizing rebreathing. Quantification of dead space is an essential tool in the treatment of patients with critical illness requiring respiratory support.</p>
      </sec>
